def reindex_layers(conn_header: ConnHeader, params: ReindexLayersParameters) -> dict:
    """
    Moves layers to new indices. Archicad cannot renumber an attribute, so each layer is recreated at its new index
    (free indices below it are filled with temporary layers, which are deleted again), gets the old layer's state in
    every layer combination, and then every element of the old layer is moved to it, in the floor plan and in every view
    and layout database, with the tool defaults on that layer. Only when the old layer is empty everywhere is it
    deleted, and the new layer gets its name, flags and folder. If an element cannot be moved (it is locked, not
    reserved in Teamwork, or comes from a hotlinked module), that layer is left as it was. The new layer has a new
    attribute id; Favorites that use the layer are not updated. Archicad keeps the shown, locked and wireframe state of
    a layer per open window: the new layer gets the old one's state in the current window and in every layer
    combination, and starts shown and unlocked in the other open windows. The model elements that sections, elevations,
    interior elevations and 3D documents show of the layer come back on the new layer when those views are rebuilt
    (listed as viewsToRebuild). dryRun lists the elements and tool defaults on each layer without changing anything. The
    whole call is one undo step. Available from Archicad 27.
    """
    return conn_header.core.post_tapir_command(
        command="ReindexLayers", parameters=params.model_dump(mode="json", by_alias=True, exclude_none=True)
    )


